// Blue fire: three in the morning inside a sulphur crater of East Java. Sulphur gas burns blue where it leaves the rock,
// molten sulphur runs red and alight down a dark slope veined with yellow sulphur to a milky, steaming acid lake.
// THE LAW OF COLOUR: four colours and a dark: electric blue fire; yellow sulphur; the teal lake; red molten threads;
// dark grey-brown rock (and pale steam). The fire's light on the land is a saturated blue: dark rock under it is a
// deep blue and sulphur a pale blue-green, never white; the sulphur's yellow is plain by the moon's and the sky's
// light and warms to yellow-orange by the melt's glow.
//
// SPACE: +X is east, +Z is south, y = 0 is the lake; the crater's middle is the origin. The bay is a horseshoe in the
// south-east wall, open north-west to the lake. Its own frame: a = metres into the bay from the top of the fire slope
// (negative toward the lake), b = metres across it toward the north-east. The rim's crest is one closed line K in plan:
// the crater's circle, with the horseshoe let into it. The land is one height law over the distance from K.
//
// THE FLIGHT, in metres of the laid line (the route table RT below holds every figure). The line is laid from out
// over the crater's outer flank, in over a saddle in the headwall at the bay's back (its crest at RT.saddle); the
// flight begins at RT.entry, inside the bay.
// THE LINE GOES ONE WAY: down the bench, over the brink and down the fire slope beside the great river of burning
// sulphur to the lake, and from the shore low over the water, through three unlike places (the open water, the
// narrows of the stand of chimneys, the burning water) to the fire fall on the crater's far wall. It never looks
// up a slope and never stands high over the lake: the fire slope is flown down 5 to 8 m over its crust, the
// lake 4.5 m over the water.
//   26-54    the arrival: 6 m inside the saddle's crest, over the headwall's inner face, the eye a
//            little down: under it a fall of burning sulphur comes down that face and runs on as a burning stream
//            down the bench, RT.runOff to the right; the crust about it burns in its cracks; the wall's face on
//            the left bears no fire of its own here; the line comes down to RT.eyeBench over the bench.
//   54-120   down across the bench and round to the right, toward the crater: the eye rests on the fire fall on
//            the crater's far wall, across the whole lake: the stream beside the line on the right, dead antler
//            trees beside it; the pool at the slope's top passes 12 m to the right near 110; over the slope's brow
//            the lake's far water with its chimneys, the far wall with its fire falls, the far rim and the stars;
//            the south-west wall's burning seams on the left; the plume's column stands far down the bay to the
//            right, over the great vents at the fire slope's foot under the north-east wall.
//   120-234  over the brink (near 120) and STRAIGHT DOWN THE FIRE SLOPE, down its fall line, RT.eyeRiver over the
//            crust's even fall, THE GREAT RIVER beside the line on the right, its middle RT.riverOff away: it
//            leaves the pool and runs burning down the slope, over each bench's riser, to the terrace and the lake.
//            The look goes down along it onto the lake's near water: the river, the terrace with its two chimneys
//            and the shore under it, the lake and its stand of chimneys before it, the far wall and the fire fall
//            over them. The south-west wall stands to the left, the west stream burning down the slope under it;
//            the buttress stands beyond the river on the right.
//   234-311  over the slope's foot and the terrace, between its two chimneys (the left one RT.chimneyOff[0] from
//            the line, the right one beyond the river), over the terrace's lip (near 270), where the river falls
//            to the lake, and down in one glide to 4.5 m over the water; the look comes up from the water to the
//            fire fall across the lake. The line first has water under it near 311.
//   311-850  LOW OVER THE WATER to the end, RT.eyeLake, through THREE UNLIKE PLACES. Each chimney of the lake's
//            stand fumes, burns at its throat and at two of its mouths, and the sulphur runs burning down its
//            flank; each stands on a shelf of yellow crust with its image in the water under it.
//   311-440  THE OPEN WATER: out from the shore a little to the right, the milky lake broad to both sides, the
//            fire fall far ahead on the crater's wall, thin falls of fire at unlike heights round the wall, in
//            its hour the old moon low over the far rim and its road on the water; low broad chimneys, middling
//            ones and a fallen one stand well apart, and near 376 the line passes through ONE GATE OF TWO
//            GIANTS, their axes 11.5 to 13.5 m from it, the look raised to their burning crowns against the sky.
//   440-690  THE NARROWS: the line turns aside to the left by 50 degrees (432 to 500), off the open water and
//            into the thickest of the stand, and threads it in two long bends, 70 degrees round to the right
//            and 45 back to the left: fourteen giants stand by turns to its two sides, their axes 11.5 to
//            15.5 m from it, so that sulphur passes close on both hands; the look rests on the line's own way
//            ahead, the far rim's deep notch and the far wall's benches before it down the first bend; the
//            stand hides the fire fall; the flight is at its slowest here.
//   690-850  THE ARCH, THE BURNING WATER AND THE FALL: at 704 the line passes under an arch of two leaning
//            giants whose crowns have grown together over it, fire along the arch's underside and falling from
//            it in drops to both sides, the look raised through it; beyond it the fire fall stands near and
//            tall, a curtain of thirty-two strands some 22 m broad at its head, the whole wall high, breaking on
//            two ledges and falling into a bowl of lit mist, its blue light on the wall's beds about it, its
//            long image in the lake; and THE LAKE ITSELF BURNS: rafts of sulphur afloat on it carry low blue
//            flames to both sides of a clear lane 14 m broad down which the line runs. Over its last RT.gateUp
//            metres the line rises to RT.eyeGate; the gate stands on the water before the far wall, the fire
//            fall RT.fallAside to its left.
//
// PLACED AGAINST THE LINE (design, held for the whole stretch named and for every deal): the saddle in the headwall's
// crest lies under the line at RT.saddle, its lowest place RT.eyeSaddle under the line; thirty of the antler trees
// stand 6 to 10 m to either side of the laid line from its second metre to RT.treesTo; the bay's plan line,
// by which the horseshoe's wall is drawn, is tied to the bench's own line (the laid line's first, straight reach)
// at BP.join; the bench's burning stream sets out under the line at RT.benchRun, on
// the headwall's inner face, falls down that face and runs down the bench RT.runOff to the line's right into the
// pool at the slope's top; the crust's cracks burn for some 20 m to that stream's right and
// 30 m to its left; THE GREAT RIVER leaves the pool and runs down the slope RT.riverOff to the line's right, from
// the brink to the terrace's lip, where it falls to the lake; the
// chimneys stand RT.chimneyOff to either side where the line crosses the terrace's lip; the stand of sulphur
// chimneys in the lake is placed one by one along the line, from where it first has water under it to 26 m short
// of the gate: one gate of two giants to either side of the line at RT.gates, their axes RT.giants[2] to
// RT.giants[3] from it; the narrows' giants by turns to the line's two sides between RT.narrows, by
// RT.narrowGiants; the arch's two giants abreast of the line at RT.arch (the slender one by RT.archSlim); between them low broad ones and middling
// ones; and six more out in the lake, 40 to 90 m from the line (THE STAND OF CHIMNEYS, below); the burning rafts
// lie to both sides of the line from RT.rafts past the arch, none within the lane; the fire
// fall stands on the crater's wall where a bearing RT.fallAside to the left of the line's last
// heading, taken from the gate, meets it, and the fallen blocks lie at its foot; the far rim's notch and the far
// wall's three benches stand where the narrows' first bend looks (RT.notch, RT.benches: bearings of the crater's
// own plan); a few giants nearest the line carry a melt run on the flank that faces it; the buttress's crown
// burns on the buttress; the plume's great vents stand 78 m and more from the line on the plan; no pool of the
// melt lies within 16 m of it;
// each moth's loop is laid about a
// point 5.5 to 9 m beside the line; the gate ends it. (The moon's bearing, the lake's springs, the far wall's seams,
// their fan, that wall's skirt of fallen rock with its three steaming vents, the bay walls' flutes, their three
// ledges and the scree at their feet, the west stream and the stream beside it under the south-west wall, and the
// sulphur buttress are facts of the crater's own plan: none is tied to the line.) The
// land's roof stands RT.rockClear (and a metre) under the line as far as the brink and RT.rockLow from there on,
// and the land keeps its clear air beside the line everywhere. Everything else is placed by the bay's
// own plan. Richness is chosen at build by place alone; nothing is placed, hidden, moved or lit for one place of the eye, a
// share of the flight, a seed's number, the visit's clock or where the eye looks.
//
// THE TWO LAWS, on the world's own clock (parts/blue-fire-crater.js, makeLaws): the wind (it swings from the north to
// the west and back; the change crosses the slope from west to east in 5 s; period 30 to 40 s) and the pour (the crust
// dam over the pool gives; the lit head runs down the main channel at 30 m/s; the channel burns, gutters and skins
// over; period 25 to 35 s). Each period is dealt by the seed; each phase is dealt at the end of the build from a fork
// of its own ('windPhase', 'pourPhase'). A visit is whole from any phase: the slope's standing fire, the older streams
// and the walls' seams burn through every phase of both.
//
// THE LIGHTS. There is no lamp in the scene: every surface is lit in its own shader (GLSL_FIRE in the part) by
//   - the night sky, from above: starlight, faint and neutral, whole on level ground, over half on a standing wall,
//     nothing under an overhang, less in a crack and in the gully of a burning line, and less the deeper a place
//     lies under the land about it (a gully of the wall, the foot of a cliff). By it alone the land far from
//     every flame is a cold charcoal with its form just seen, and a thing shows its tone and little of its hue.
//     THE NIGHT IS DARK: by the sky and the hour's key light together bare rock stands at a tenth to a fifth of
//     the screen's white and sulphur at a quarter to a third, so that whatever burns is brighter than the ground
//     it stands on; nothing lies in black: the walls' relief, the rim against the sky and the terrace are seen;
//   - THE KEY LIGHT OF THE HOUR, one low light from the sky with a bearing and a height, fixed at the build. In
//     the old moon's hour it is the moon itself, which stands 23 degrees up over the crater's far rim beyond the
//     lake (its bearing is the way from the bay to the crater's middle and 13 degrees on: a fact of the lake's
//     lie); in the moonless hour it is the sky's own pale band over that same rim, 16 degrees up and half as
//     strong; before dawn it is the east's first light, 12 degrees up, a dull rose. It rakes the land, the walls,
//     the towers, the stones and the plants by their own normals, a wall by the plane of its face. ITS SHADOW IS
//     LAID ONCE AT THE BUILD: the land's height over a square kilometre, the towers stamped into it, is walked
//     away from the light, and what the walk leaves is the height the light clears at every place; a place under
//     that height is in shadow (by the land that stands three squares of that walk and more up the light from
//     it: a wall's small relief shades by its own faces). So a gully is dark, a buttress's flank lit, and the
//     crater's crest, dark against the sky, takes a thin pale rim all round the bowl from the sky's own glow,
//     brighter on the side the light comes from, so that the bowl's whole round is read against the night. WHAT
//     STANDS PROUD OF THE LAND ABOUT IT (a crest, a riser's edge, a hummock, a ledge's lip, a stone's top and
//     edges) takes that rim too, also when the ground about it lies just in shadow, and a little of the
//     sky's own light from above. AND THE KEY LIGHT GLANCES OFF THE OPEN GROUND: seen against it, a road of
//     pale sheen lies over the ground toward the light's bearing, on the tops of plates, stones and
//     hummocks, broken by every crack (this reads the eye's way: shading);
//   - THE WALLS' OWN FORM, WHICH EVERY LIGHT FINDS: a wall stands in slabs with a dark joint down between two
//     and each slab a tone of its own; in beds (the ring's great beds 24 m thick, the bay walls' 17 m), each
//     with a darker foot and a pale lip; in gullies, whose beds are dark and whose edges are pale; and each
//     slab is broken into courses 2.5 to 12 m deep and these into blocks 3 to 6 m long, every block's face
//     turned a little its own way and of its own tone, with dark joints between: the courses of one slab do not
//     run on into the next, so a bed is broken at every joint, and a light that comes along the wall or from
//     below lies on one block and misses its neighbour;
//   - THE FIRE, WHICH LIGHTS THE CRATER. Its light on the land, the stones, the towers and the walls is a
//     SATURATED BLUE (0.08, 0.38, 1.0 times its strength), multiplied with the colour of what it falls on: dark
//     rock under it is a deep blue with its cracks dark in it, sulphur a pale blue-green. IT IS THE LOCAL LIGHT
//     OF THE SLOPE: about every field of fire lies a soft pool of it without an edge, and out of the pools the
//     ground falls back to the night's tone. IT IS BOUNDED: what it lights bends over softly and ends near 0.11
//     of brightness in linear light's measure, under half of the screen's white and plainly blue, also hard
//     by a flame or a burning river: the fire itself is the only near-white thing. The melt's warm light is
//     bounded for itself and added: where the two meet the ground is violet. It comes four ways:
//       the flames' own short light, laid at build into the fire's map: whole within 2 m of a flame, a third
//       at 6 m, a tenth at 10 m, nothing by 20 m, taken most by a face turned to the flame (the way to it is
//       the slope of that map) and little by the face turned away, so that a stone or a hummock throws its
//       dark side away from the fire; a tower takes it from the flames at its foot, and it is gone some 15 m up;
//       the fire's wide light, from the same map: how much burns within some 30 m in plan; on the open ground
//       half of it is left 15 m beyond the last line and a sixth at 40 m, so the ground between two rivers
//       is lit from both. ITS DIRECTION IS THE SLOPE OF THE FIRE'S OWN MAP (the way up that map is the way
//       to where most burns): open ground takes it low from that side, so a face turned to the fire is blue
//       and a face turned from it is in shadow, by the ground's larger form: hummocks, plates, risers and
//       stones show their shape by it; a wall takes it from below, and a third of it is left 35 m over the
//       bay's floor;
//       the fire slope's far light: the bay's walls stand in it across the bay's whole breadth, each face by
//       how it is turned to the places that burn, and the bay's open floor (the bench, the terrace) takes
//       it thinly from the side the slope stands on;
//       ten fixed places (three on the slope, two in the glowing gas over it, three down the main channel, two among the
//       walls' seams), each strength set every frame from the two laws alone: inside the bay the walls take them
//       as a thin wash from below, most at a wall's foot and a third of it left 35 m up; outside the bay the rim,
//       the land beyond and the crater's far wall take them thinly and, more thinly, their far light, on the faces turned
//       to the bay; the bay's floor takes only the lower of the gas's two, thinly, from above;
//       and the rim of it along an edge seen edge-on (the towers, the walls, the plants);
//   - the burning lines' own map, laid at build: along every burning line (the cracks down the slope, the pools
//     where they end, the vents) the ribbon of burning ground, blue (it is flame), its pattern sliding downhill on
//     the world's clock; the thread of molten sulphur in the crack, red-orange; that thread's WARM light on the
//     crust for some 3 m to either side. It is a map of the ground's plan; a wall's seam, which runs down the
//     wall's fall, is seen by it too.
//     Where molten sulphur lies broad (a pool at a line's end) it is a small calm glowing body, deep red,
//     orange where it wells up, in a rim of dark crust. A RIVER'S BED (the great river, the bench's stream,
//     the older streams) is dark rock with a crust of yellow sulphur along both banks, lit by the fire that
//     burns over it: its fire is the tongues and sheets that stand on it; a lesser stream carries one
//     red-orange thread of the melt down its middle; the melt's warm light reaches
//     some 4 m onto its banks, and its red glow further (two fifths of it 6 m from a bank, a seventh at
//     12 m), low over the ground from the melt's side, so the face of a stone or a hummock that is turned
//     to the river is warm; and the gas burns over it in low flames that come and go;
//   - the fire's map of the bay, laid at build: a wall's light from its own burning seam, which comes along the
//     face and from below: strong within some 5 m of the crack, and beyond that a broad soft wash on the wall
//     for 10 to 30 m to either side, the broader the taller the seam; the dull red of the rivers, a third of it
//     left 6 m from a bank and a tenth at 12 m; and how much
//     sulphur has gathered at each place (it maps the fire: an apron along every burning line, about every
//     vent and pool, some 4 m broad along the rivers' banks, round the buttress's foot; where it ends it has a
//     lip with a dark line under it). The stones on the floor read the same maps where they stand (the short
//     light, the wide light, the sulphur's dust) and take them on the faces turned to the fire, so that each
//     has a lit side and a dark side; a channel's crusted lip is dark with ash and takes little of them;
//   - a far wall's seam, laid into the wall's own corners: each lies in a gully of its own; its light on the
//     gully's flanks and on the wall some 15 m to either side, most on the faces turned to it, and its blue glow
//     in the gas. The greatest of them is the fire fall (on the far wall, by the gate): it comes down a hollow
//     some 90 m broad as a curtain of thirty-two strands of burning sulphur side by side, 22 m broad at its
//     head and broader under each of the two ledges it breaks on, to a burning fan on the water and a bowl of
//     lit mist; its blue lies on the wall for some 40 m to either side of it and on the
//     skirt of fallen rock below; the lake's steam, faintly teal up the foot of the wall round it. The thin
//     falls round the wall, each straight down its own gully, light the rock about them by the wall's own
//     corners alone; every other one ends part of the way down in a small pool of fire on a bed's tread.
//     THE ROCK CARRIES NO PAINTED FIRE: a seam's fire and the fall's are the strokes and tongues that stand
//     before the rock; the rock is lit by them, softly and evenly, and in a seam's gully shows only a faint
//     breath of blue in the gas. At the fire fall's foot fallen blocks lie at the
//     water's edge, lit blue on the side turned to the fall (a place 12 m up it), dark on the other;
//   - THE WARM LIGHT OF THE WATER LEG, rare and small: on each of the far wall's three benches a small pool of
//     molten sulphur with a few thin runs down the face under it, low on that wall a broader melt to either
//     side of the fire fall, 15 to 19 m over the water, just above its foot's steam, and on the flank of at most five giants of
//     the stand one thin melt run: red and orange, glowing of itself, lighting the rock and the yellow for a
//     few metres about it warm. Never a band and never a pattern;
//   - each burning fan's light on the water about it; a chimney's crown on the collars under it, and on a chimney
//     of the lake's stand the light of its two burning mouths on the skin about and under each; the small
//     flames of the buttress's five ledge vents on the pipes and lobes about them (whole within a metre, a third
//     at 2.2 m, nothing by 5.5 m: laid into the crag's own corners), and the crown that burns on the buttress's
//     broken top on the crag's head, from above, for some 10 m down;
//   - the smoke and the steam: grey by the night sky, each puff paler on the side turned to the place it rose
//     from and darker on the other, and light blue from below for its first 12 m over the fire; the plume's great
//     bodies light blue from below for their lowest 30 to 40 m, a dark grey above with a paler crown, and paler
//     than the sky on the side turned to the low moon or the east's first light; the lake's banks of steam teal;
//   - THE FLAME IS WRITTEN AS LIGHT, in linear light's own measure: a flame's body 1.5 to 2.5 in its blue,
//     its small heart 3 to 3.5 and paler, a spark to 3.3, the melt's brightest cracks and threads 2 and over; and the engine's bloom
//     (strength 0.2, radius 0.25, threshold 1.0, the same in every hour) spreads what stands over 1.0 into a
//     close halo, a few pixels about a bright heart, and lays no veil over the frame: the ground, which stands
//     far under 1.0, is left alone;
//   - THE AIR (GLSL_AIR in the part; the table AIR below): the bowl is full of fume, thick at the lake's level and
//     thinning upward, thicker over burning ground. It has a light of its own, the night sky's, a neutral slate a
//     little paler than unlit rock: so a thing is the paler and the softer the further off it stands, never the
//     darker. Where a look passes over burning ground the fume in it glows the fire's blue; low over the lake,
//     faintly, the lake's teal; close over the melt a little dull red. Away from every fire it is neutral;
//   - the lake's glow, from the lake's own place: the water is a milk of sulphur that glows a pale turquoise
//     from within: pale milk in the shallows by the shore and round each stack, a deeper and darker blue-green
//     toward its middle and under the far wall (on its far half it glows two thirds as much), and over each of
//     its six hot springs a paler upwelling that turns slowly on the world's clock; the same teal lights the
//     crater's wall from below over its lower third, fading upward, and the steam up the walls' feet, and at
//     the bay's mouth the terrace, the lower slope and the stones there on the side turned to the lake; on its
//     face pale yellow sulphur foam lies only as a soft raft some metres broad in the lee of each stack and as
//     a few windrows piled along the lee shore, and the open water carries none; the sky's light, the wall's dim image,
//     the image of FOUR of the far wall's seams, each as tall as its seam and dimmer than it (the lake holds four
//     places for a seam's image, and the first four far seams that are laid take them: the wall's three old seams,
//     the short one round to the south-west, the long one straight across the lake from the bay and the one round
//     to the north-east, and the fire fall by the gate; the thin falls round the wall, which are laid after them,
//     have no image, unless one of those four is not laid, when the first thin fall laid takes its place), each
//     chimney's image in its own yellow and its crown's, and
//     in its hour the key light's road, broken by the ripples, toward the moon's or the dawn's own bearing.
// None of them follows the eye; none is made or switched after the build; the key light, its shadow and the hour
// are settled in build() and stand for the whole visit.
//
// WHAT READS WHERE THE EYE IS, THE LENS OR THE SCREEN, and nothing else does. Each is optics, shading or a filter of
// detail, the same law at every place; none places, hides, moves, times or lights a thing.
//   THE AIR reads the eye's place and how far off a thing stands: it is the optics of the fume between the two (how
//   much of the thing it hides, how much of its own light and of the fire's it adds on the way), the same law for
//   the land, the lake, the towers, the stones, the plants, the flyers, the flames, the sparks and the steam.
//   THE LAKE'S MIST reads them likewise, for the land, the chimneys, the lake's face and the stones: so much of the
//   mist as lies along the straight line from the eye's place to the thing, thin near the eye and thick far off (see
//   MIST), its own light the stronger where that line runs toward the hour's light.
//   THE FIRE, THE SMOKE AND THE SPARKS are the part's, and the part's head names each of their readings. In short: a
//   flame's tongues, a seam's and a fall's strokes, a halo, a puff's square and a spark's streak are turned to the
//   eye's place; a burning line's ribbon seen edge-on shows nothing; a halo and a chimney's fire take their depth a
//   little nearer the eye's place than they stand; a puff's lit side is laid across its square as it is seen from
//   there; and each is filtered by the pixel's footprint at its distance (what a pixel cannot hold is left out or
//   drawn the fainter).
//   THE LENS: this world's field of view (FOV, below) is handed to the engine for the eye and to the part, which
//   makes of it and of the drawing's height in pixels the pixel's footprint a metre away, for those filters of the
//   part's. The world's own shaders take a pixel's footprint from how a place changes from one pixel to the next.
//   THE LAKE is in part a mirror (shading). By the way a look from the eye's place glances off the water it shows
//   the sky, the wall, the four far seams' images, the images of the fire's ten fixed places, the fans' and the stacks'
//   images and the key light's road (more of the mirror and less of the water's own glow the lower the look); it
//   lays each burning fan's image down toward the eye's place, as long as the fan's low flames would be seen from
//   the eye's height over the water, and each burning raft's likewise; the bars into which the ripples break those
//   images lie across the look, told off by the water's distance from the eye's place on the plan; and it shows
//   more or less of its glow by how a ripple's face is turned to the eye. BY THE PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT it reads its
//   ripples (a size of ripple is left out where a pixel is longer than its crests lie apart), its glitter's spread
//   and its foam's streaks, AND THE RAFTS' PLATES: a plate's edge and the lead beside it are drawn no finer than a
//   pixel, a plate's burning rim is as much dimmer as a pixel is broader than it, and where a plate is less than a
//   pixel deep the pack is drawn as its own mean.
//   THE LAND (the towers are of its mesh). Shading: rock, towers and walls that face a fire take a thin rim of its
//   light where they are seen edge-on on the way to the eye's place; the key light's sheen on the open ground, the
//   waxy sulphur's sheen and a tower's crystal glints are taken toward the eye; and from how a place changes across
//   the screen the land takes the plane of a wall's face and the side of a far seam a face is turned to. BY THE
//   PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT it filters its own detail: the rock's grain, crumbs and chips, the hair cracks of the crust's
//   plates and the crack between two plates (never thinner than a pixel), the sulphur's needles and sparkle, how
//   broad a lobe of set sulphur is drawn, a river's running pattern (read at the level a pixel asks) and the
//   flicker of a burning line's sheet of light (even where a pixel covers a third of a metre and more). AND ON
//   THE WALLS, each named: THE BLOCKS of a slab and their joints are not drawn where a pixel covers a third of a
//   block; THE SLAB JOINTS (never thinner than a pixel) and each slab's own tone are not drawn where a pixel covers
//   a third of a slab; THE BED SHADE, the dark foot and the pale lip of each bed (the ring's beds 24 m thick, the bay
//   walls' 17 m), fades from where a pixel covers a seventh of a bed's height and is not drawn where it covers
//   two fifths of it.
//   A STONE takes the planes of its faces and its edges from how its surface changes across the screen, filters its
//   grain, pits and chips by the pixel's footprint, and takes the fire's rim where it is seen edge-on and the key
//   light's sheen toward the eye (shading).
//   A PLANT takes the fire's rim along its edges on the way to the eye's place, and A PLANT'S BACK FACE, a sheet seen
//   from behind, takes a turned normal (shading only: which side of the sheet the eye is on).
//   THE SKY'S DOME rides with the eye.
//   THE LOOK AND THE PACE are read off where the eye IS along the line: the engine hands the flight's share, the
//   world turns it into the laid metre the eye stands at, and every place of the look (each rest, and each window in
//   which the look goes from one rest to the next) and of the pace is in laid metres of the route table. One thing
//   alone is set by the flight's share itself: how great a share the look has, which grows over the flight's first
//   0.04 and goes out between 0.93 and 0.985 of it. THOSE THREE NUMBERS MIRROR THE ENGINE'S OWN GUARD, by which the
//   engine lets every world's look go at a flight's two ends: the world must know what share the engine will give
//   its look in order to set the rest so far round that the look as flown is the one composed. They are the
//   engine's numbers, not places of this world.
// BY PLACE, AT BUILD, THE SAME FOR EVERY VISIT (nothing of the eye is read in either): THE MIST'S LANE: the lake's mist
// is thinner by 0.88 of itself within 18 m to either side of the line's last reach (the straight way from the laid
// line's place where the burning water begins to the gate; a third of the thinning is left 18 m out to its side),
// coming in between 210 and 150 m short of the gate, and within 32 m about the gate; from 4 to 30 m past the gate,
// on toward the fall's foot, it goes back to the whole, and within some 14 m of the fall's foot the bowl of mist is
// never thinned. THE POOLS: no pool of the melt is made within 16 m of the laid line (taken at every second metre of
// it from its first to the shore; the pools lie on the bay's floor).
// The key light, its shadow, the moon's road's bearing and every fire's light on things read nothing of the eye.
// No animal answers the eye.
//
// DRAWS: every kind of thing has a fork of its own ('seams', and of it 'fall' (the fire fall) and 'thin' (the thin
// falls); 'fire'; 'towers', and of it 'stand' (the lake's stand); 'noise'; 'rafts'; 'plantshapes'; 'crystals' (the
// buttress's stalactites); 'sparks'; 'steam', and of it 'stones'; 'fallBlocks'; 'plants'; 'flyers'; 'springs' (the
// lake's hot springs); 'hour'; 'windPhase' and 'pourPhase'); the two laws' periods and the sky's stars are drawn
// from the seed's own trunk. A puff, a spark, a drip, a stalactite, a plant, a stone or a flyer that would stand
// too near the line is not made, its draws still taken, so that leaving it out shifts nothing that is dealt after
// it. A FLAME IS THE EXCEPTION:
// a tongue that is left out has drawn its own seed, and the draws of its soft lights are NOT taken; nor are the
// draws of a ribbon's soft lights at a metre of the ribbon where the clear air leaves it no height or where it does
// not burn. What the fire's fork deals after such a place is therefore other than it would be were the tongue
// made. Which tongues are left out is settled by place at the build, so a deal is the same on every visit. The
// hour is dealt from 'hour' and changes no other draw: a visit has the same land, fire and life in every hour.
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { makePath } from '../kit/core.js';
import { makeSky } from '../kit/sky.js';
import { buildHero, makeFlyers, flyerCourse, makeLaws, makeNoiseTexture, GLSL_NOISE, GLSL_LAWS, GLSL_FIRE, GLSL_AIR } from '../parts/blue-fire-crater.js';

const D2R = Math.PI / 180, FOV = 62;                               // (FOV: the field of view this world hands the engine, degrees)
const sat = (x) => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, x));
const sstep = (a, b, x) => { const t = sat((x - a) / (b - a)); return t * t * (3 - 2 * t); };
const mix = (a, b, t) => a + (b - a) * t;

// ---------- THE AIR: the fume that fills the bowl (its law is GLSL_AIR in the part) ----------
const AIR = {
  thick: 0.0022,       // how much of a thing one metre of fume hides at the lake's level (a level look of 100 m at 20 m up hides a tenth: across the lake the wall and the water are seen to meet)
  high: 28,            // metres up in which the fume thins to a third
  vent: 0.012,         // as much again for each metre of the look that passes over ground lit whole by the fire
  over: 24,            // metres over the bay's floor in which the fume of burning ground thins to a third
  glow: 0.03,          // how readily that fume glows: a look through 23 m of it shows half of the fire's light in the air
  slate: 0.25,         // the fume's own light, as a share of the night sky's light on level ground
  fire: [0.003, 0.013, 0.034], // the fire's light in the air, whole: the same saturated blue as on the ground, and thin
  teal: 2,             // the lake's light in its steam, as a multiple of the lake's own glow
};
// ---------- THE LAKE'S MIST: the lake is warm and the night cold, and a thin mist lies on the water in long low
// sheets that drift down the wind. It is light in the air and has no body: like the bowl's fume it is taken in closed
// form along the straight line from the eye's place to the thing seen (THIS READS THE EYE'S PLACE AND THE DISTANCE:
// it is the optics of air, the same law at every place), so a thing near the eye is seen through next to none of it
// and the far shore through the most. It lies only over the lake, thickest at the water's face and thinning to a
// third every MIST.high metres up: a chimney's foot stands in it and its crown clear over it; it is thicker toward
// the crater's far wall, whose foot it veils; its sheets are long down the wind and narrow across it and slide on
// the world's clock. Its own light is the hour's: pale toward the moon or the sky's bright side, and blue about
// the fire fall's foot. The land, the chimneys, the lake's face and the stones are seen through it; a flame is
// its own light and is not veiled ----------
const MIST = {
  thick: 0.004,        // how much of a thing one metre of mist hides at the lake's face, where a sheet of it lies
  high: 5,             // metres up in which it thins to a third
  far: [0.4, 1.5],     // how thick it lies at the bay's shore and under the far wall, as shares of that
  sky: 0.3,            // its own light: so much of the night sky's light on level ground
  moon: 0.9,           // and so much of the hour's light on steam
  toKey: 1.4,          // as much again of both where the look goes toward the hour's light
  blue: 0.8,           // the fire fall's blue in it, within some 70 m of the fall's foot
  bowl: 5,             // about the fall's foot it stands so many times as thick as a sheet of it
  lane: [0.88, 18, 32], // along the line's last reach over the burning water and about the gate the mist is thinner by this share: within so many metres to either side of that reach (a third of it left at that distance), and so many metres about the gate; from the gate on toward the fall's foot it stands whole again, and within some 14 m of the foot itself the bowl is never thinned
};
const GLSL_MIST = /* glsl */`
uniform vec4 uMist, uMistFall, uMistDir, uMistLane; uniform vec3 uMistCol, uMistBlue; uniform float uMistT; uniform sampler2D tMist;
// (mistAt answers: rgb the light the mist sends to the eye from along the line, a how much of the thing is still seen.
// The line's share of mist by height is taken in closed form; where its sheets lie is read at three places along it)
vec4 mistAt(vec3 P){
  vec3 E = cameraPosition, D = P - E; float L = length(D);
  float yE = max(E.y, 0.) * uMist.y, yP = max(P.y, 0.) * uMist.y, dy = yP - yE;
  float through = L * (abs(dy) > 1e-3 ? (exp(-yE) - exp(-yP)) / dy : exp(-yE)), k = 0., blue = 0.;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { vec3 Q = E + D * (.4 + .27 * float(i)); vec2 w = vec2(Q.x * .8 + Q.z * .6, Q.z * .8 - Q.x * .6);
    float n = textureLod(tMist, vec2(w.x * .0031 - uMistT * .0009, w.y * .011), 0.).r * .65 + textureLod(tMist, vec2(w.x * .009 - uMistT * .0021, w.y * .031) + .37, 0.).g * .35;
    // (THE LANE AND THE GATE STAND IN THINNER MIST: along the line's last reach, down the lane of the burning
    // water, and about the gate at its end, the sheets and the bowl are thinner by MIST.lane's share; beyond
    // the gate, toward the fall's foot, the bowl stands whole. uMistLane: the gate's place and the way the
    // lane runs to it; t: metres past the gate along that way, s: metres to its side)
    vec2 dl = Q.xz - uMistLane.xy; float t = dot(dl, uMistLane.zw), s = dl.y * uMistLane.z - dl.x * uMistLane.w;
    float thin = 1. - ${MIST.lane[0].toFixed(2)} * (1. - smoothstep(4., 30., t)) * max(exp(-s * s / ${(MIST.lane[1] * MIST.lane[1]).toFixed(1)}) * smoothstep(-210., -150., t), exp(-dot(dl, dl) / ${(MIST.lane[2] * MIST.lane[2]).toFixed(1)}));
    k += (1. - smoothstep(282., 312., length(Q.xz))) * mix(uMist.z, uMist.w, smoothstep(-.7, .9, dot(Q.xz, vec2(-.70711)) / 300.)) * (.25 + 1.5 * smoothstep(.3, .72, n)) * thin;
    // (and about the fire fall's foot the mist stands thick, a bowl of it some 45 m across, lit by the fall)
    // (within some 14 m of the foot itself the bowl is whole wherever the gate stands)
    vec2 df = Q.xz - uMistFall.xy; float bowl = exp(-dot(df, df) / 2000.) * mix(thin, 1., exp(-dot(df, df) / 200.)); k += uMistFall.w * bowl; blue += uMistFall.z * (exp(-dot(df, df) / 5000.) + bowl); }
  float T = exp(-uMist.x * through * k / 3.), toKey = pow(max(dot(D.xz, uMistDir.xy) / max(length(D.xz), 1e-3), 0.), 3.);
  return vec4((uMistCol * (1. + uMistDir.z * toKey) + uMistBlue * (blue / 3.)) * (1. - T), T); }
`;

